===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əˈkeɪ.ʃə/|/əˈkeɪ.sjə/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/əˈkeɪ.ʃə/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-acacia.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪʃə|s=3}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{bor|en|la|acacia}}, from {{der|en|grc|ἀκακία||shittah tree}}, either from {{der|en|ine-pro|*h₂eḱ-||sharp}} (compare {{m|grc|ἀκή||point}}) or more likely a {{der|en|qsb-grc}} word. First attested before 1398. {{doublet|en|cassie}}.
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₂eḱ-}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~|s|acaciae}}

# {{lb|en|countable}} A [[shrub]] or [[tree]] of the tribe {{taxfmt|Acacieae|tribe}}. {{defdate|First attested around 1350 to 1470.}}<ref name=SOED>Brown, Lesley, ed. ''The Shorter Oxford English Dictionary.'' 5th. Oxford: Oxford University Press, 2003.</ref>
#* '''1997''', Kenneth M. Old, Ian A. Hood, Zi Qing Yuan, ''Diseases of Tropical '''Acacias''' in Northern Queensland'', K. M. Old, Su Lee See, J. K. Sharma (editors), ''Diseases of Tropical '''Acacias''': Proceedings of an International Workshop held at Subanjeriji (South Sumatra) 28 April - 2 May 1996'', [http://books.google.com/books?id=oDVrua2oTZAC&pg=PA1&dq=%22acacia%7Cacacias%22+-intitle:%22acacia%22&hl=en&ei=-c6fTpqsG9PUiAKdy_1j&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=2&ved=0CDIQ6AEwAQ#v=onepage&q&f=false page 1],
#*: The latter species was collected only once in this survey on ''A. flavescens'' but is widespread on both tropical and temperate '''acacias''' in Australia.
# {{lb|en|uncountable|pharmacy}} The thickened or dried juice of several species in {{taxfmt|Acacieae|tribe}}, in particular {{taxfmt|Vachellia nilotica|species}} (syn. {{taxlink|Acacia nilotica|species}}, {{vern|Egyptian acacia}}). {{defdate|First attested around 1350 to 1470.}}<ref name=SOED>Brown, Lesley, ed. ''The Shorter Oxford English Dictionary.'' 5th. Oxford: Oxford University Press, 2003.</ref>
# A [[false acacia]]; {{vern|robinia tree}} ({{taxfmt|Robinia pseudoacacia|species}}). {{defdate|First attested in the mid 17th century.}}<ref name=SOED/>
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} [[gum arabic|Gum arabic]]; [[gum acacia]]. {{defdate|First attested in the early 19th century.}}<ref name=SOED/>
# {{lb|en|loosely}} Any of several related trees, such as a [[locust tree]].
# A light to moderate greenish yellow with a hint of red.{{colour panel|E3D733}}

